Chapter: ImportGate
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt

 This chapter is in the foreword, and it is in the foreword on purpose.
 The compiler sits to the left of codex.os and of every app quire, so it
 cannot reach the trust lattice or the repository protocol. If the
 compiler is to refuse untrusted code -- and that is the whole claim --
 the refusal has to live somewhere the compiler can see.

 So the work is split. Computing trust is a rich, expensive thing: a
 vouch graph, transitive scores, decay. That stays in codex.os.trust
 where it already lives. Enforcing trust is a small, cheap thing: does
 this content hash to the hash I asked for, is it signed by a key I
 hold, does that key score high enough. That is all this chapter does,
 and it needs nothing but hashes, signatures, and a table.

 The table is a TrustManifest: fingerprint to public key and score,
 materialised by whoever did the expensive part. The repository protocol
 builds one from its lattice. The compiler will read one from disk. The
 gate does not care which, and cannot tell the difference -- which is the
 point.

 We say:

Section: Signed Facts

 A Fact is content-addressed but anonymous: its hash binds its content
 and nothing else. A SignedFact binds an author to it. The signature
 covers the content hash together with the metadata the hash does not
 reach -- kind, author, timestamp -- so neither the body nor its
 attribution can be changed without breaking the signature.

  SignedFact = record {
    fact : Fact,
    signature : List Integer,
    signer-fingerprint : Text
  }

  fact-signing-content : Fact -> Text
  fact-signing-content (f) =
    f.hash & "|" & fact-kind-name (f.kind) & "|" & f.author & "|" & show (f.timestamp)

  sign-content-bytes : List Integer, List Integer, Text -> List Integer
  sign-content-bytes (priv) (pub) (content) =
    ed25519-sign priv pub (sha256 (text-to-bytes content))

  verify-content-signature : List Integer, Text, List Integer -> Boolean
  verify-content-signature (pub) (content) (sig) =
    ed25519-verify pub (sha256 (text-to-bytes content)) sig

  verify-fact-signature : List Integer, SignedFact -> Boolean
  verify-fact-signature (pub) (sf) =
    verify-content-signature pub (fact-signing-content (sf.fact)) (sf.signature)

Section: The Trust Manifest

  TrustedKey = record {
    public-key : List Integer,
    score : Integer
  }

  TrustManifest = record {
    keys : HamtMap TrustedKey
  }

  manifest-empty : TrustManifest
  manifest-empty = TrustManifest { keys = hamt-empty }

  manifest-add : TrustManifest, Text, List Integer, Integer -> TrustManifest
  manifest-add (m) (fingerprint) (pub) (score) =
    TrustManifest {
      keys = hamt-set (m.keys) fingerprint (TrustedKey { public-key = pub, score = score })
    }

  manifest-lookup : TrustManifest, Text -> Maybe TrustedKey
  manifest-lookup (m) (fingerprint) = hamt-get (m.keys) fingerprint

Section: The Fact Bundle

  FactBundle = record {
    facts : BareFactStore,
    signed : HamtMap SignedFact
  }

  bundle-empty : FactBundle
  bundle-empty = FactBundle { facts = fact-store-empty, signed = hamt-empty }

  bundle-publish : FactBundle, SignedFact -> FactBundle
  bundle-publish (b) (sf) =
    let f = sf.fact
    in FactBundle {
      facts = fact-store (b.facts) f,
      signed = hamt-set (b.signed) (f.hash) sf
    }

 A fact filed with no signature beside it. The gate already knows what to
 do with one -- it answers ImportUnsigned -- but until there was a way to
 offer a work unsigned, that answer could not be reached, and a
 diagnostic that cannot fire is a promise the compiler does not keep. An
 unsigned work is not a work signed badly: bundle-publish would file a
 signature that fails to verify and the gate would call it forged, which
 is a true sentence about the bytes and the wrong answer to the question.

  bundle-offer : FactBundle, Fact -> FactBundle
  bundle-offer (b) (f) =
    FactBundle {
      facts = fact-store (b.facts) f,
      signed = b.signed
    }

Section: The Gate

 Four guards, in order, and any guard that fails refuses the import
 outright:

   first, the fact must be present.
   then, its content must hash to the hash that was asked for.
   then, it must carry a signature that verifies against a key we hold.
   finally, the signer's score must reach the threshold.

 The order matters. Integrity is checked before the signature is
 consulted, because a signature over a lie is still a lie; and the
 signature is checked before the score, because an unverifiable signer
 has no score worth reading.

  ImportOutcome =
    | ImportOk (Fact)
    | ImportMissing
    | ImportCorrupt
    | ImportUnsigned
    | ImportUnknownSigner
    | ImportForged
    | ImportUntrusted (Integer)

  ImportResult = record {
    local-name : Text,
    outcome : ImportOutcome
  }

  import-result : Text, ImportOutcome -> ImportResult
  import-result (local-name) (outcome) =
    ImportResult { local-name = local-name, outcome = outcome }

  gate-import : FactBundle, TrustManifest, Integer, Text, Text -> ImportResult
  gate-import (bundle) (manifest) (threshold) (hash) (local-name) =
    when fact-load (bundle.facts) hash
      is None -> import-result local-name ImportMissing
      is Just (f) -> gate-check-integrity bundle manifest threshold f hash local-name

  gate-check-integrity : FactBundle, TrustManifest, Integer, Fact, Text, Text -> ImportResult
  gate-check-integrity (bundle) (manifest) (threshold) (f) (hash) (local-name) =
    if fact-content-hash (f.content) == hash
    then gate-check-signed bundle manifest threshold hash local-name
    else import-result local-name ImportCorrupt

  gate-check-signed : FactBundle, TrustManifest, Integer, Text, Text -> ImportResult
  gate-check-signed (bundle) (manifest) (threshold) (hash) (local-name) =
    when hamt-get (bundle.signed) hash
      is None -> import-result local-name ImportUnsigned
      is Just (sf) -> gate-check-signer manifest threshold sf local-name

  gate-check-signer : TrustManifest, Integer, SignedFact, Text -> ImportResult
  gate-check-signer (manifest) (threshold) (sf) (local-name) =
    when manifest-lookup manifest (sf.signer-fingerprint)
      is None -> import-result local-name ImportUnknownSigner
      is Just (tk) -> gate-verify-signature threshold sf tk local-name

  gate-verify-signature : Integer, SignedFact, TrustedKey, Text -> ImportResult
  gate-verify-signature (threshold) (sf) (tk) (local-name) =
    if verify-fact-signature (tk.public-key) sf
    then gate-check-trust threshold sf tk local-name
    else import-result local-name ImportForged

  gate-check-trust : Integer, SignedFact, TrustedKey, Text -> ImportResult
  gate-check-trust (threshold) (sf) (tk) (local-name) =
    if tk.score >= threshold
    then import-result local-name (ImportOk (sf.fact))
    else import-result local-name (ImportUntrusted (tk.score))

Section: Reading the Verdict

  import-accepted : ImportResult -> Boolean
  import-accepted (r) = when r.outcome
    is ImportOk (f) -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  import-outcome-name : ImportOutcome -> Text
  import-outcome-name (o) = when o
    is ImportOk (f) -> "ok"
    is ImportMissing -> "missing"
    is ImportCorrupt -> "corrupt"
    is ImportUnsigned -> "unsigned"
    is ImportUnknownSigner -> "unknown-signer"
    is ImportForged -> "forged"
    is ImportUntrusted (score) -> "untrusted"
